  "query_preview": "..High pressure centered near the Four Corners area\ntoday will shift east for the weekend. This will turn the flow\nover Utah to more southwesterly, allowing some drier air to spread\ninto the area. The mean PWAT from the NBM dips to below 0.9 inches\nacross portions of southwest and central Utah on Saturday, with\nthese values spreading into northern Utah on Sunday. This would be\na change from the 1-1.3 inches analyzed the area this evening. As\na result, expect convective coverage to decrease for Saturday and\nbecome confined mostly to near the mountains and across southwest\nUtah. Even though the airmass will be drier for Sunday, models\nsuggest a weak vort max rotating around the high will lift across\nsoutheast Utah. This will provide some dynamics which could bring\nincreased convective coverage to portions of southern Utah. \n\nMoisture looks to increase back into the area late Monday into\nTuesday as the flow aloft transitions back to a more southerly\ncomponent as the ridge amplifies and starts drifting back towards\nthe Four Corners. Expect convection to increase in coverage again\nthrough around Thursday as a result. Thereafter, the high \npressure is progged to drift west towards northwest AZ and \nsouthwest UT towards the end of the week. This would then cut of \nthe moisture and bring a drier airmass into the area. \n\nWith a strong ridge in place, temperatures are expected to be\nunseasonably hot, particularly for the valleys of northwest Utah,\nincluding the Wasatch Front, as well as the Uinta Basin. Maxes are\nexpected to rise as high as the lower 100s through Monday, with \nthe monsoon moisture keeping overnight lows elevated. For large \nportions of northwest Utah, lows are expected to stay in the mid \nto upper 70s, providing little relief from the heat overnight. \nBecause of the anticipated increased risk of heat related illness \nin this pattern, a Heat Advisory is in effect for the Wasatch \nFront, northwest Utah, the Cache Valley, and the Uinta Basin. \nThe return of moisture by Tuesday is expected to moderate \ntemperatures some, though they will remain around 5F above normal\nfor northern portions of the area.",
